From 80e898be362254ec3db7480f446ecc5ed167baa3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: keyspark <kevin.on.the.rocks@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 15:10:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] GB10: shared-staged int4 weight loads for iq2/q2K MoE and
 q8 GEMV kernels

The iq2_xxs (66 B), q2_K (84 B), and q8_0 (34 B) block strides defeat load
vectorization: per-block scalar loads hold the decode MoE kernel at ~110
GB/s where the same access pattern with int4 loads reaches ~170 (measured
against a 226 GB/s sequential ceiling on GB10; microbench in the campaign
notes).  Each 8-lane oct (or warp for q8) now stages a whole 16-byte-aligned
weight row into its own shared slot with __ldcs int4 loads and decodes from
shared, preserving per-lane block sets and reduction order.

Staged variants: decode gate/up LUT, fused sum6 down, per-pair batch
gate/up + down (2-16 row speculative verifier batches, where the expert-tile
dedup wins little: adjacent draft rows share ~2 of 6 experts), q8 warp8 /
mrow / pair GEMVs.  Escape hatches: DS4_CUDA_MOE_NO_STAGED,
DS4_CUDA_NO_STAGED_Q8.

GB10 measured: decode routed_moe 0.384 -> 0.258 ms/layer; plain decode
14.9 -> 16.2 tok/s; verify(2) 93 -> 82 ms; DSpark full config on the code
bench 16.6 -> 19.0 tok/s.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
